
  <sn><md>### Concept of Free Energy

**Definition:**
Free energy (Gibbs free energy, G) is the **energy available to do useful work** in a system at constant temperature and pressure. It determines whether a reaction will occur **spontaneously** and predicts the **direction** of chemical reactions in soil and aquatic systems.

---

**Gibbs Free Energy Equation:**

$$
\Delta G = \Delta H - T\Delta S
$$

Where:
- **ΔG** = change in free energy
- **ΔH** = change in enthalpy (heat content)
- **T** = absolute temperature (Kelvin)
- **ΔS** = change in entropy (disorder)

---

**Spontaneity of Reactions:**

| ΔG Value | Meaning | Reaction Direction |
|---|---|---|
| **ΔG &lt; 0** | Energy released | **Spontaneous** (exergonic) |
| **ΔG = 0** | At equilibrium | No net change |
| **ΔG &gt; 0** | Energy required | **Non-spontaneous** (endergonic) |

---

**Standard Free Energy (ΔG°):**
Measured at standard conditions (25°C, 1 atm, 1 M concentration).

$$
\Delta G = \Delta G^\circ + RT \ln Q
$$

Where:
- **R** = gas constant (8.314 J/mol·K)
- **T** = temperature (K)
- **Q** = reaction quotient

At equilibrium, ΔG = 0 and Q = K:
$$
\Delta G^\circ = -RT \ln K
$$

---

**Relationship with pE:**

$$
\Delta G^\circ = -nF \Delta E^\circ
$$

Where:
- **n** = number of electrons transferred
- **F** = Faraday constant (96,485 C/mol)
- **ΔE°** = standard electrode potential

Also:
$$
\text{pE}^\circ = \frac{\Delta G^\circ}{2.303nRT}
$$

**Higher pE° → more negative ΔG° → more spontaneous reaction.**

---

**Importance in Soil and Aquatic Systems:**

1. **Predicts reaction direction:** Tells whether a reaction will proceed spontaneously.
2. **Redox sequence:** Microorganisms use electron acceptors in order of **decreasing energy yield** (O₂ &gt; NO₃⁻ &gt; Mn⁴⁺ &gt; Fe³⁺ &gt; SO₄²⁻ &gt; CO₂) based on ΔG values.
3. **Nutrient transformations:** Determines which forms of N, S, Fe, Mn are stable.
4. **Mineral stability:** Predicts weathering and formation of clay minerals.
5. **Microbial metabolism:** Microbes derive energy from reactions with negative ΔG.
6. **Pollutant degradation:** Determines whether pollutants can be broken down spontaneously.

---

**Example:**
Aerobic respiration:
$$
\text{CH}_2\text{O} + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow \text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \quad \Delta G^\circ = -475 \text{ kJ/mol}
$$
Highly negative ΔG° → very spontaneous → microorganisms prefer O₂ as electron acceptor.

Sulfate reduction:
$$
\text{SO}_4^{2-} + 2\text{H}^+ + 4\text{H}_2 \rightarrow \text{H}_2\text{S} + 4\text{H}_2\text{O} \quad \Delta G^\circ = -152 \text{ kJ/mol}
$$
Less negative ΔG° → less energy → used only when O₂ and NO₃⁻ are absent.

---

**Summary Table:**

| Concept | Meaning |
|---|---|
| ΔG &lt; 0 | Spontaneous, energy released |
| ΔG &gt; 0 | Non-spontaneous, energy needed |
| ΔG = 0 | Equilibrium |
| ΔG° = -RT ln K | Links free energy to equilibrium constant |
| ΔG° = -nF ΔE° | Links free energy to electrode potential |
| More negative ΔG° | More spontaneous, higher pE° |

---

**Conclusion:**
Free energy (ΔG) determines the **spontaneity and direction** of chemical reactions in soil and aquatic systems. It is linked to **equilibrium constant (K)**, **electrode potential (E°)**, and **pE**. Microorganisms follow a **redox sequence** based on decreasing free energy yield, controlling nutrient cycling, mineral stability, and pollutant behavior.</md></sn>
